# Revisions git blame should look through rather than at.
#
# A commit that only reformats code makes `git blame` useless: every line points
# at the reformat instead of at the change that introduced it. Listing the commit
# here restores the real answer.
#
# Enable it once per clone (it is a local setting, not a repository one):
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub reads this file automatically in its blame view.
#
# Rules for adding a line
#   - The commit must change formatting and nothing else. If it also fixes a bug,
#     the bug fix becomes invisible to blame — split it first.
#   - Full 40-character SHA. Abbreviated hashes are rejected by git.
#   - One commit per line, with a comment saying what it reformatted.
#
# PENDING: backend/src has not been formatted yet. It predates .prettierrc.json
# by roughly 13,000 lines of whitespace, and CI enforces formatting only on the
# files a change touches (the `format` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml) so the
# formatted share grows instead of arriving in one commit nobody can review.
# When that reformat is finally run — `make format`, on its own branch, with no
# other change in it — add its SHA below.
